.he "cpp (1)                     10/01/26                      cpp (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cpp
- the standalone C preprocessor
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
cpp [ -PRC ] [ -D name [ = value ] ] [ -U name ] [ -I directory ]
.br
    [ input [ output ] ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Cpp reads C source and writes it with #include files inserted and
macros expanded, reading standard input and writing standard output
unless file names are given.  It is a filter and a program of its
own, not a pass of the C compiler; the compiler is ccc and its front
end is /libexec/pass0, which is not this program.
.sp
With an input file named, cpp reads that file instead of standard
input.  With an output file named as well, it writes there instead of
standard output.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FLAGS
.in +5
.br
.bd
-D name [ = value ]
.sp
Define name as 1, or as value.  A bare -D with no name is ignored.
.sp
.bd
-U name
.sp
Remove an initial definition of name.
.sp
.bd
-I directory
.sp
Add directory to the include search path.  It is searched before
/usr/include.
.sp
.bd
-P
.sp
Do not write the # line "file" lines that mark the start of an
included file.
.sp
.bd
-R
.sp
Allow a macro to expand itself.  Without it, runaway recursion is
reported as an error.
.sp
.bd
-C
.sp
Pass comments through to the output instead of deleting them.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is John F. Reiser's preprocessor from the seventh edition, the
one its own notes call five to twelve times faster than the one it
replaced.  Symbols given with -D are defined as 1, exactly as if they
had been written #define name 1; -D name = value defines name as
value.
.sp
The name cpp is shared with the compiler's front end by accident.
ccc runs its front end as pass0, out of /libexec, and pass0 is the
lexer and normalizer, not a preprocessor this command can be mistaken
for.  The two are separate programs; see the README in the source
directory.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1)
